<script>
  const TOPICS = [
    { id: 'features', title: 'Features overview' },
    { id: 'how', title: 'How it works' },
    { id: 'config', title: 'Config files' },
    { id: 'processes', title: 'Processes' },
    { id: 'routing', title: 'Routing' },
    { id: 'logs', title: 'Logs' },
    { id: 'cron', title: 'Scheduled jobs' },
    { id: 'hooks', title: 'Deploy hooks' },
    { id: 'audit', title: 'Audit & history' },
    { id: 'health', title: 'Health & alerts' },
    { id: 'cli', title: 'CLI' },
    { id: 'keys', title: 'Keys' },
  ]

  class {
    init() {
      this.state.topic = 'features'
      this.state.topics = TOPICS
    }

    select(id) {
      this.state.topic = id
      if (this.state.pane) this.state.pane.scrollTop = 0
    }
  }
</script>

<div class="page-header">
  <div>
    <p class="page-pretitle">Help</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Operator guide</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="page-actions">
    <button class="btn" type="button" onclick="Drawer.showReference()">
      <svg class="icon"><use href="#i-book"></use></svg>
      Annotated reference
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card help">
  <nav class="list-group help-nav" aria-label="Help topics">
    {#each state.topics as topic}
      <button class="list-group-item" class:active={topic.id === state.topic} type="button" onclick="fez.select('{topic.id}')">
        <strong>{topic.title}</strong>
      </button>
    {/each}
  </nav>

  <div class="help-body" fez:this="pane">
    {#if state.topic === 'features'}
      <section class="help-features">
        <h2>Features overview</h2>
        <p>What appboss gives you, by area. Each heading jumps to the full guidance.</p>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('processes')">Supervision</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Process groups from a <code>procfile</code>; the <code>web_process</code> starts first, then the rest by name.</li>
          <li>One fixed <code>PORT</code> per app and process type, injected with <code>APP_NAME</code>, <code>PROC_TYPE</code> and <code>APPBOSS_SOCKET</code>.</li>
          <li><code>autostart</code> and <code>running.json</code> resume exactly the apps that should be up across host restarts.</li>
          <li><code>idle_stop</code> stops an idle app; the next request wakes it with a starting page and <code>Retry-After</code>.</li>
          <li><code>health</code> checks (<code>tcp</code> or <code>http:&lt;path&gt;</code>) with <code>health_interval</code> and <code>health_timeout</code>; the web process is polled for its whole lifetime.</li>
          <li>Restart policy (<code>restart</code>, <code>max_restarts</code>, <code>restart_backoff</code>) and a <code>crashed</code> state that alerts; <code>unhealthy_threshold</code> restarts a web process that fails its liveness checks.</li>
          <li>Graceful drain on stop and restart: new requests get <code>503</code> while in-flight ones finish within <code>stop_timeout</code>.</li>
          <li>Optional cgroup v2 limits (<code>resources</code>, <code>memory_max</code>, <code>cpu_max</code>); falls back to process groups elsewhere.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('routing')">Routing &amp; proxy</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Host routing from <code>hosts</code>, including <code>*.</code> subdomains and <code>.example.com</code> for the apex plus every subdomain.</li>
          <li><code>canonical_host</code> 301s every other host; <code>maintenance</code> serves a page while the app keeps running.</li>
          <li>Access control with <code>basic_auth</code> (bcrypt via <code>appboss password</code>) and <code>allow_ips</code> CIDRs.</li>
          <li>Static files from <code>static</code>, with immutable caching for chosen prefixes (<code>static_immutable</code>).</li>
          <li>Response <code>headers</code> and a request body limit (<code>max_body</code>).</li>
          <li>Cloudflare-first: <code>trusted_cidrs</code> and <code>client_ip_headers</code>; forwarded headers are added only when missing.</li>
          <li>Tunable upstream timeouts and keep-alive pooling.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('logs')">Logs &amp; search</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>One SQLite store per app with <code>REQUEST</code>, <code>STDOUT</code>, <code>appboss</code> and one channel per <code>*.log</code> file.</li>
          <li>Full-text search over messages and raw lines, with level and request filters.</li>
          <li>Full-screen viewer at <code>/logs</code> with shareable URLs and export; <code>appboss logs</code> tails or queries the same store.</li>
          <li>Rotation and ingestion of process logs, <code>log_retention</code> and <code>stdout_retention</code>, a daily prune and SQLite <code>VACUUM</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('cron')">Scheduled jobs</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>cron:</code> one-shot commands on <code>every &lt;n&gt;&lt;s|m|h|d&gt;</code> or a five-field expression, even while the app is stopped.</li>
          <li>Per-job <code>timeout</code> and <code>overlap</code>; each run logs to its own <code>cron-&lt;job&gt;</code> channel.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('hooks')">Deploy hooks</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>hooks:</code> one-shot commands triggered by a signed ping to <code>/hooks/&lt;app&gt;/&lt;hook&gt;</code>.</li>
          <li>Auth by a generated or configured secret: query token, bearer, GitLab token or GitHub HMAC.</li>
          <li><code>restart: true</code> rolls the app after a clean run; <code>appboss exec</code> runs one-off commands in the same environment.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('audit')">Audit &amp; history</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Every mutation is audited with its actor: console email, <code>hook:&lt;app&gt;/&lt;hook&gt;</code> or <code>cli</code>.</li>
          <li>Config history keeps the last 50 revisions per file and restores one, revision-checked.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('health')">Health &amp; alerts</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>/healthz</code>, <code>/readyz</code> and Prometheus <code>/metrics</code> on the management host, optionally token-gated.</li>
          <li>A public per-app <code>health_endpoint</code> that never wakes a stopped app.</li>
          <li>A debounced operator webhook (<code>notify</code>) for crash, restart-loop, health-timeout, wake-failed, hook-failed, deploy and config-changed.</li>
          <li><code>appboss doctor</code> preflights tools, directories, the config and the port range.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('cli')">Console &amp; CLI</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Management console with live state, start, stop, restart and maintenance controls, a validated config editor, logs, audit and help.</li>
          <li>The Sys tab inspects the host read-only: OS, kernel, load, memory, disks and the installed toolchains (Node, npm, Bun, Ruby, Python and more) with their versions.</li>
          <li>AuthCog sign-in for admins, plus one-time <code>appboss login</code> links over the control socket for local work.</li>
          <li>Full CLI over the control socket, with <code>--json</code> on every command.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><a class="help-jump" onclick="fez.select('config')">Config</a></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>One <code>appboss.yaml</code>, two roles: a host with <code>apps:</code>, an app with <code>procfile:</code>; <code>appboss.local.yaml</code> wins for server-only values.</li>
          <li><code>defaults:</code> applies to every app and is overridden key by key, once more per process under <code>processes.&lt;name&gt;</code>.</li>
          <li><code>$NAME</code> expands from the daemon environment; <code>procfile</code> and cron commands are never expanded.</li>
          <li>Validate with <code>appboss check</code>, inspect with <code>appboss config [app] -d</code>, browse every key in the live reference.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'how'}
      <section>
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <p>One <code>appboss</code> process owns the ports, the proxy and the console. Each app is a folder with an <code>appboss.yaml</code>; its <code>procfile</code> maps a process name to a command. Boss gives every process a fixed <code>PORT</code> and routes each request to the app whose <code>hosts</code> match.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A stopped app starts on the first request and answers with a starting page that refreshes.</li>
          <li>An idle app stops after <code>idle_stop</code> and wakes the same way.</li>
          <li>Stop and restart drain first: new requests get <code>503</code> while those in flight finish, bounded by <code>stop_timeout</code>.</li>
          <li>Every mutating action is written to the audit log, and crash and failure events can post to one operator webhook.</li>
          <li>Cloudflare is the edge: TLS, caching, compression and WAF live there. appboss is the origin and trusts only Cloudflare's ranges.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre>browser -> Cloudflare -> appboss :80
                          | Host shop.example.com -> shop (PORT 3101)
                          | Host api.example.com  -> api  (PORT 3102)</pre>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'config'}
      <section>
        <h2>Config files</h2>
        <p><code>appboss.yaml</code> has two roles. A file with <code>apps:</code> is a host; a file with <code>procfile:</code> is an app. Never both. <code>appboss.local.yaml</code> next to a host file wins over it, is gitignored, and is where server-only values (secrets, admin emails, absolute paths) live.</p>
        <p><code>defaults:</code> in the host file applies to every app. An app's top-level key overrides it key by key, and <code>processes.&lt;name&gt;</code> overrides it once more for a single process.</p>
        <p>A <code>$NAME</code> in a value is replaced from the daemon's environment before it loads, so <code>url: $ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL</code> keeps the secret out of the file. Only all-uppercase names expand, an unset name is left as written, and <code>procfile</code> and cron commands are never expanded because they are runtime shell lines.</p>
        <h3>Host</h3>
        <pre>apps: ./apps
proxy:
  listen: ":80"
ports:
  range: [3100, 3199]
defaults:
  idle_stop: 30m</pre>
        <h3>App</h3>
        <pre>procfile:
  web: ./start.sh
hosts: [shop.example.com]
health: http:/up</pre>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'processes'}
      <section>
        <h2>Processes</h2>
        <p>Each key under <code>procfile</code> is a process type. All of them run at once; only <code>web</code> gets proxied traffic. Boss injects <code>PORT</code> (fixed per app and process type), <code>APP_NAME</code>, <code>PROC_TYPE</code> and <code>APPBOSS_SOCKET</code> into every process.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>autostart: false</code> keeps an app down until <code>appboss run</code>, the console, or a request starts it.</li>
          <li><code>health</code> is the readiness check: <code>tcp</code>, or <code>http:&lt;path&gt;</code> expecting a 2xx. A process that never passes it is restarted.</li>
          <li>After the web process is ready the check keeps running every <code>health_interval</code>; <code>unhealthy_threshold</code> consecutive failures restart it (<code>0</code> disables the ongoing check).</li>
          <li><code>idle_stop</code> stops an app with no traffic; <code>0s</code> disables it.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre>procfile:
  web: bundle exec puma -p $PORT
  worker: bundle exec sidekiq
autostart: false
health: http:/up
health_interval: 500ms
idle_stop: 0s</pre>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'routing'}
      <section>
        <h2>Routing</h2>
        <p>Every app lists the hostnames it answers in <code>hosts</code>. The proxy reads the request <code>Host</code>, picks the app, and forwards to its <code>web</code> process. Non-web processes never get traffic.</p>
        <p>A leading <code>*.</code> matches subdomains only; a leading <code>.</code> matches the bare domain and every subdomain, so <code>hosts: .example.com</code> is <code>example.com</code> plus <code>*.example.com</code>. A single value is accepted like a list.</p>
        <p><code>maintenance on</code> answers every request with the maintenance page while the app keeps running. A stopped app gets the starting page, then the real response once it is healthy.</p>
        <pre>hosts:
  - shop.example.com
  - www.shop.example.com

proxy:
  client_ip_headers: [CF-Connecting-IP, X-Forwarded-For]
  trusted_cidrs:
    - 173.245.48.0/20</pre>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'logs'}
      <section>
        <h2>Logs</h2>
        <p>Each app has one SQLite store at <code>log_dir/&lt;app&gt;/appboss.sqlite</code>. The full-screen viewer at <code>/logs</code> and <code>appboss logs</code> read the same data.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>REQUEST</code> - one row per proxied request.</li>
          <li><code>STDOUT</code> - the stdout and stderr of each process.</li>
          <li><code>appboss</code> - the daemon's own log.</li>
          <li>one channel per <code>*.log</code> file under the app's <code>log/</code> folder.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><code>log_retention</code> (default two weeks) covers requests and app log files; <code>stdout_retention</code> (default three hours) covers stdout and the daemon log. <code>log_retention: 0</code> turns the store off for the app. A daily job also runs SQLite <code>VACUUM</code> on every store at <code>daemon.vacuum_at</code> (default 04:30).</p>
        <pre>appboss logs -f shop
appboss logs shop --process worker -n 500
appboss logs shop --search timeout --level error --channel stdout</pre>
        <p>With <code>--search</code>, <code>--level</code> or <code>--channel</code> the command queries the store instead of tailing files.</p>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'cron'}
      <section>
        <h2>Scheduled jobs</h2>
        <p><code>cron:</code> declares one-shot commands that run even while the app is stopped. <code>schedule</code> is <code>every &lt;n&gt;&lt;s|m|h|d&gt;</code> or a five-field cron expression. Jobs run in the app folder with the app environment and log to their own <code>cron-&lt;job&gt;</code> channel.</p>
        <pre>cron:
  cleanup:
    schedule: every 6h
    command: bundle exec rake cleanup
    timeout: 30m
    overlap: false</pre>
        <p><code>appboss cron shop</code> lists jobs and the next run; <code>appboss cron run shop cleanup</code> starts one now. The app card in the console has a Run button.</p>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'hooks'}
      <section>
        <h2>Deploy hooks</h2>
        <p><code>hooks:</code> declares one-shot commands a signed HTTP ping triggers, so a Git host webhook can deploy without shell access. The ping URL is <code>&lt;management.url&gt;/hooks/&lt;app&gt;/&lt;hook&gt;</code>.</p>
        <p>Authentication is the hook's own secret, accepted as <code>?token=</code> in the URL, <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>, <code>X-Gitlab-Token</code>, or a GitHub <code>X-Hub-Signature-256</code> HMAC over the raw body. With no <code>secret</code> in the config, appboss generates one under <code>state_dir</code> on first use. <code>appboss hooks</code> prints the ready-made URL and <code>appboss hooks rotate</code> mints a new one.</p>
        <pre>hooks:
  deploy:
    command: git -C .. pull --ff-only
    timeout: 10m
    restart: true
    overlap: false</pre>
        <p><code>appboss exec shop bundle exec rails runner ...</code> runs a one-off command in the same environment and prints its combined output; options like <code>--timeout</code> come before the command. Both hooks and cron write to their own log channel.</p>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'audit'}
      <section>
        <h2>Audit &amp; history</h2>
        <p>Every mutating action records who did what to which app and how it turned out: start, stop, restart, maintenance, rescan, cron and hook runs, <code>exec</code>, and config writes and restores. Console actions carry the signed-in email, a hook ping carries <code>hook:&lt;app&gt;/&lt;hook&gt;</code>, and control-socket actions are attributed to <code>cli</code>.</p>
        <p>Rows live in the reserved <code>_appboss</code> database, are kept for <code>daemon.audit_retention</code> (default one year, <code>0</code> forever) and are pruned with the daily log prune. The Audit tab filters by app, actor and action; <code>appboss audit</code> prints the same rows.</p>
        <p>Every config save first copies the current file to <code>state_dir/config-history</code>, keeping the last 50 revisions per file. The Configuration view's History button lists them and can restore one, revision-checked; <code>appboss config history|restore</code> does the same from the CLI.</p>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'health'}
      <section>
        <h2>Health &amp; alerts</h2>
        <p>The management host serves three endpoints when <code>management.metrics.enabled</code> is true (the default):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>GET /healthz</code> - <code>200 ok</code> while the daemon is up.</li>
          <li><code>GET /readyz</code> - <code>200</code> only while every <code>autostart</code> app is running, else <code>503</code>.</li>
          <li><code>GET /metrics</code> - Prometheus text: per-app state, memory, CPU, restart and request rates, latency quantiles and the last cron and hook runs. Set <code>management.metrics.token</code> to require a bearer token.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each app also answers on its own hosts at <code>health_endpoint</code> (default <code>/.well-known/appboss/health</code>): <code>200</code> while running and not draining, <code>503</code> otherwise. It runs before auth and never wakes a stopped app. <code>appboss doctor</code> preflights a box: tools, writable directories, a valid config and a clear port range.</p>
        <p><code>notify:</code> posts runtime events to one operator webhook: <code>crash</code>, <code>restart-loop</code>, <code>health-timeout</code>, <code>wake-failed</code>, <code>hook-failed</code>, <code>deploy</code> and <code>config-changed</code>, debounced per app and event. An empty <code>url</code> disables it.</p>
        <pre>notify:
  url: $ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL
  events: [crash, restart-loop, health-timeout, wake-failed]
  min_interval: 5m</pre>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'cli'}
      <section>
        <h2>CLI</h2>
        <p>Remote commands talk to the running host over its control socket and accept <code>--json</code>. Inside an app folder the app argument defaults to that app. Run <code>appboss help &lt;command&gt;</code> for one command.</p>
        <h3>Host session</h3>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>start</code><span>run the host in the foreground; Ctrl-C stops every app</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>systemd</code><span>print the systemd unit, or install and enable it</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>kill</code><span>stop every app and clear every listener in ports.range</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>login</code><span>print a one-time console URL that signs you in as cli@localhost</span></div>
        <h3>Apps</h3>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>ls</code><span>list apps with state, ports, uptime, activity and memory</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>run</code><span>start an app, rescanning first when it is not known yet</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>stop</code><span>stop an app and keep it stopped until run or the next request</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>restart</code><span>stop and start an app on the same ports</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>status</code><span>processes, restarts, resources and request rates for one app</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>logs</code><span>print or follow the process logs; <code>-f</code> follows</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>maintenance</code><span>serve the maintenance page while the app keeps running</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>cron</code><span>list an app's scheduled jobs, or run one now</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>hooks</code><span>list an app's deploy hooks, run one, or rotate its secret</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>exec</code><span>run a one-off command in the app's environment</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>audit</code><span>list operator actions: start, stop, restart, hook runs and config writes</span></div>
        <h3>Config</h3>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>config</code><span>validate and print a config, the resolved config, keys, or saved revisions</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>check</code><span>validate the config and every app without starting anything</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>doctor</code><span>preflight a box: tools, writable directories, valid config, clear ports</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>rescan</code><span>re-read the apps directory and every appboss.yaml</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>ports</code><span>show the live port table, one fixed port per process</span></div>
        <div class="help-cmd"><code>password</code><span>print a bcrypt hash for basic_auth</span></div>
      </section>
    {/if}

    {#if state.topic === 'keys'}
      <section class="help-keys">
        <h2>Keys</h2>
        <p>Every key, its type, its default (or an example) and where it applies. Use the filter to narrow by name or description; the annotated reference has the long form.</p>
        <ab-config-keys />
      </section>
    {/if}
  </div>
</div>
